    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d814">

                <pb facs="0847" n="745" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg745"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.9412B/3: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Japan (Grew)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-06-15T13:00:00-04:00">June 15, 1933—1 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>60. Press despatches from Tokyo have stated (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) that
                    Viscount Ishii and President Roosevelt agreed in principle that an arbitration
                    treaty be concluded between the United States and Japan and (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) that Japanese Ambassador is negotiating on that
                    line with Department. So far as Department is able to ascertain, there is no
                    basis in fact for (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>); and (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >b</hi>) is absolutely untrue.</p>
                <p>It is noted that latest press reports from Tokyo state that Ishii is going to
                    take this subject up with Secretary Hull in London.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
